n2. Add an offline entry point, proposed as `tools/cfd/part.mjs`. Have `node tools/cfd/part.mjs wing reference` read the two base/flap pairs, sample the probe, and write the 28 mm display crop under the ignored `tools/cfd/out/wing-reference/`. Refactor the raw reader out of the existing exporter if sharing it. Importing it must have no write side effects. Keep the original whole-car commands usable. [Volume layout and reader](../tools/cfd/export.mjs)\n3. In a raw 3-D file the four Float32 blocks are density, rearward velocity, lateral velocity and upward velocity. For `N = nx * ny * nz`, the cell offset is `(k * ny + j) * nx + i`. Its centre in mm is `[x0 - (i + 0.5)*h, (j + 0.5)*h, (k + 0.5)*h]`. Check for `16*N` bytes of field data and `N` mask bytes. Average the rearward and upward velocities over **x −3920…−3750, y 200…300, z 1230…1290 mm**. Compute `atan2(meanUp, meanRearward)` in degrees. Expect 48 fluid cells on the fine grid and 12 on the coarse grid, with the four angles above. Use actual rearward flow as the arrow’s horizontal direction.\n4. Recreate the figure’s 28 mm crop by selecting cell centres in **x −4300…−3150 and z 810…1420 mm**, at **y = 238 mm**. This yields 41 columns × 22 rows. Its first column / bottom row centre is `[-3171, 238, 826]` mm. Colour by vertical speed divided by inlet speed, on the same −0.25…+0.25 range for both cases. Read the flap and boot outlines from the selected solid mask. Keep the full-precision volume for deriving the overlaid arrow and paths. [Reference image](evo-wing-flow-comparison.png)\n\n### B. Build one component player and prove it with the wing\n\n**Ownership.** Adapt `subsystems/race-aero.js` and its Evo configuration together. It already owns the precomputed case controls, theme mapping and tracer drawing, and the Evo is its only current caller. Reuse the same subsystem for each new part. Leave `subsystems/aero.js` serving the other stops. The story already supplies a step and an orbit target for each component. [Component loader and visibility](../lib/app/viewer.js) · [Story and disclosure layout](../lib/app/story.js) · [Current CFD player](../subsystems/race-aero.js)\n\n**File contract.** Use one `field.json` and one `<case-id>.bin` per state under `stops/<slug>/cfd/<part>/`. Start with one scalar field per part. The manifest declares the plane, location, cell spacing, dimensions, scalar quantity, shared display range and case IDs. Each case carries its computed paths and guides in vehicle mm. Here is the wing reference header to use in the first round-trip test.\n\n```json\n{\n  \"plane\": \"xz\",\n  \"originMm\": [-3171, 238, 826],\n  \"cellMm\": 28,\n  \"width\": 41,\n  \"height\": 22,\n  \"quantity\": \"vertical-speed-over-inlet\",\n  \"range\": [-0.25, 0.25],\n  \"cases\": {\n    \"base\": { \"lines\": [], \"guides\": [] },\n    \"flap\": { \"lines\": [], \"guides\": [] }\n  }\n}\n```\n\nRows run from bottom to top. Advancing a column always decreases vehicle x. Advancing a row increases z for `xz` or y for `xy`. The origin is the **centre of the first cell**. Use vertical velocity divided by inlet speed for the wing, pressure coefficient for the splitter, and mean-flow speed divided by inlet speed for the wheel views. Each binary is exactly `width * height` bytes in row-major order. Reserve 255 for solid. Quantize the common scalar range into 0…254 with rounding and clamping. Draw solid cells opaquely so the moving part remains legible. A path is an ordered array of `[x,y,z]` points. Give each guide a `kind` of `arrow` or `line` and a `points` array in the same frame. The fixture above illustrates the header. The display assets must contain the measured guide and the paths that the lesson uses. Keep labels in translated stop data. Keep run provenance, input geometry, both-grid results and raw units with the generation tools.\n\n1. Start with `lib/model/part-field.js` and `tests/unit/part-field.test.mjs` for the pure format/mapping logic used by the exporter and 