tionary tyres over the moving road and label that simplification in both languages. The 3-D kernel supports a moving-wall term, but the current stepped spinning tyre pumped air artificially. A rotation-specific claim needs a resolved surface, contact treatment and a successful comparison to a moving-ground wheel benchmark. Match physical tyre-road clearance across the two grids. [Wheel modelling note](../tools/cfd/geometry.mjs) · [Experimental wheelhouse flow study](https://www.mdpi.com/1996-1073/12/17/3340)\n4. **W 196 R.** Use one matched front wheel with the local exposed and enclosed body shapes. Choose a wheel-height `xy` plane and a downstream station in common exterior air, clear of both solids and the outlet. Fix these in physical mm before evaluating the final pair. As a starting definition, mark the connected band behind the shoulder where mean speed is below 80% of the inlet speed. Record its two lateral edges and width. Use the same threshold and search interval in both cases. Trace air from identical seed positions, export both outlines and wake guides, and have the text describe the observed direction. If the width is ambiguous, inspect the displacement and shape of the wake on the same plane. If neither repeats across grids, revise the domain or measurement and leave this comparison pending.\n5. **Evo arch.** Seed a short ribbon just outside the front shoulder. In the fitted-arch case, export its path round the flare into the flank and measure its lateral displacement at a fixed station. For a comparison, change only the outer arch contour around the same tyre. Verify actual clearance on both grids. If the mask remains identical or the wake shift is unresolved, ship the validated fitted-arch path as a single-state lesson. **Follow the air** highlights that path and gives its one-sentence explanation. Label the narrower arch as an illustrative design experiment.\n\n**Numerical acceptance for new runs.** Save the physical domain, spacing, boundary conditions, inlet speed, viscosity, settling time, sample region and reference velocity with each result. After the startup transient, take at least three successive time-window means spanning several flow-through times in total. In 3-D, `fields()` is cumulative over averaged steps. Difference successive weighted sums or accumulate the few local probes by window. Check finite fields, mask alignment and distinct local geometry, then compare the local observable across the two grids, time windows and an enlarged domain. Publish a directional comparison when its sign repeats and exceeds the observed window scatter on each grid. For a wake contour, repeat the check on its position or width and inspect the field itself. Keep the local result attached to the part. Whole-car drag, axle load and top speed each require a whole-car calculation.\n\n### E. Integrate, localize and test the final stops\n\n- **Stop data.** Use Evo component IDs `wing`, `splitter`, `wheel-arch`, then its existing `engine`. Use W 196 R component IDs `wheel`, then its existing `engine`. Point all four flow steps at `race-aero` with distinct data folders and component-specific `view`, `prompt`, `answer`, `more` and `talk`. Use `params.data` for the precomputed assets. The existing `params.flow` belongs to the live tunnel and its tests expect `params.shapes`. Move the Evo road figures into `note`, and label any W 196 R top-speed comparison as illustrative. Update the ledes, caveats, and sources to match each final run. Merge the aero edits into the current EN and HU arrays to preserve the W 196 R’s in-progress engine changes. Check remaining asset references before retiring the old whole-car display files.\n- **Translations.** Mirror the English component order in both stops’ `hu.js` files. Keep component-specific `params.cases.*.label` and `.say` in these overlays. Use literal `t(\"…\")` calls for common controls and loading states and add the matching catalog keys. For a new text field extend the translation test’s `TEXT` list. Keep case IDs independent of displayed strings. Check selection and pause after switching EN → HU → EN. [Translation coverage](../tests/unit/helpers.mjs) · [Catalog checks](../tests/unit/i18n.test.mjs)\n- **Browser regressions.** The generic smoke test currently expects an `output` on every step and clicks the first case. With baseline selected that click does nothing. For the `race-aero` subsystem, assert the ready result, tap the *other* case, then assert the pressed state and changed result. For a single-state arch, tap **Follow the air** and check its visible result. Keep the moving-readout assertion for the other subsystems. Add a focused check for an actual stage change with the camera fixed and playback paused. Compare the same patch of fluid above the wing or downstream of the wheel, outside both solid masks and away from buttons. Also decode each exported field in the unit test and verify that the guide points in the direction of the recorded local result. A change in the bodywork alone must fail this check. Cover an immediate A → B → A switch, leaving/re-entering the step, reduced motion, failure/retry and language replay. [Smoke suite](../tests/e2e/smoke.spec.mjs) · [Step helpers](../tests/e2e/helpers.mjs)\n- **Build and offline.** The build recursively includes `stops/` and stamps the worker with a content hash. Keep raw CFD outside shipped folders. Inspect the stamped precache for every new manifest and binary, and report the combined new byte count against the earlier Evo payload of 236,084 bytes. Extend the Chromium offline test to wait for **Ready offline** on the index, disable networking, cold-open both stops, enter every new step and exercise their controls. Wait for a case-specific result and inspect the changed field with networking disabled. [Build script](../tools/build.mjs) · [Offline test](../tests/e2e/offline.spec.mjs)\n\nRun the following from the repo root. The new unit-test path and component IDs become available during implementation. The existing Playwright configuration starts a built site on port 8766. The optional screenshot helper uses the development server on 8765.\n\n```sh\nnode --test tests/unit/flow.test.mjs tests/unit/part-field.test.mjs tests/unit/configs.test.mjs tests/unit/i18n.test.mjs\nnpm run e2e -- --project=iphone --grep '190e-evo2|w196r'\nnpm run e2e -- --project=chromium-offline\nnpm test\nnpm run e2e\n```\n\nFor comparable desktop captures, start `npm run serve` in a second terminal. After B, use the proposed English button labels in the captures.\n\n```sh\nnode tools/stop-shot.mjs 190e-evo2 wing /tmp/evo-wing-base.png 1000 'Pause airflow'\nnode tools/stop-shot.mjs 190e-evo2 wing /tmp/evo-wing-raised.png 1000 'Flap raised' 'Pause airflow'\n```\n\n**Final handoff.** Return one paired paused image per two-state lesson, one annotated path for a single-state arch, the two-grid observations, exact generation commands, input provenance, offline byte count and test results. On physical iPhone and iPad, check portrait and landscape, both themes, Hungarian wrapping, reduced motion and a cold offline opening. Ask a visitor to point to the change in the air with the details closed. Record which checks ran on hardware and which ran in emulation. If a local solve remains unresolved, identify the affected lesson and its last verified result.","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false},"serverClassifierContext":{"request":"5f982e97-cedb-4c31-8c1c-b40a1d8ecade","context":{"git_state":{"cwd":"/Users/kunabel/workspace/MercedesExperience","root":"/Users/kunabel/workspace/MercedesExperience","branch":"main","default_branch":null,"status":null,"visibility":{"origin":{"host":"github.com","remote":"abelkun-androniq/MercedesExperience","visibility":"public"},"push_remote":"origin","remotes":[{"name":"origin","host":"github.com","remote":"abelkun-androniq/MercedesExperience","visibility":"public"}],"visibility_cache":[{"host":"github.com","remote