"Flap raised: in the same box the air now rises, about 4° upward, and the paths behind the wing bend up with it.\" },\n58:      id: \"splitter\", label: \"Front splitter\", subsystem: \"race-aero\",\n69:          { id: \"in\", label: \"Lip in\", say: \"SPLITTER_IN\" },\n70:          { id: \"forward\", label: \"Lip forward\", say: \"SPLITTER_FORWARD\" },\n76:      id: \"wheel-arch\", label: \"Front wheel arch\", subsystem: \"race-aero\",\n86:        cases: [{ id: \"fitted\", label: \"Flared arch\", say: \"ARCH_SAY\" }],\n91:      id: \"engine\", label: \"Racing engine\", subsystem: \"inline-engine\",\n7://   evo-arch solve   3-D front corner of the Evo with its flared arch, 24 and 16 mm\n147:  \"evo-arch\": { slug: \"190e-evo2\", cases: [\"fitted\"], plane: 344, station: -600, seeds: [700, 740, 780, 820, 860, 920, 1000, 1100].map(y => [1500, y, 344]),\n151:const BIG = { \"w196-wheel\": { x0: 3000, x1: -2600, Y: 2600, Z: 2000 }, \"evo-arch\": { x0: 2367, x1: -2600, Y: 2600, Z: 2200 } };","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false,"bashEditDiff":{"files":[{"filePath":"/Users/kunabel/workspace/MercedesExperience/stops/190e-evo2/car.js","hunks":[{"oldStart":31,"oldLines":57,"newStart":31,"newLines":61,"lines":["   },","   components: [","     {","-      id: \"race-aero\", label: \"Racing aerodynamics\", subsystem: \"race-aero\",","-      prompt: \"Why would a racing driver accept more drag? Raise the wing flap and compare the arrows with the baseline.\",","-      answer: \"In this model, raising the flap presses the rear axle down by about 34 kg more at 200 km/h (25 to 43 kg on its two grids) and costs about 9 kW more of the engine's power in drag (4 to 14 kW); the front axle hardly changes. The extra rear load is grip and stability in fast bends, the drag is speed lost on the straights: the circuit decides which matters more. These are the simplified model's figures, not the real car's.\",","-      view: { pos: [3.2, 4.6, 2.7], target: [-1.5, 0, 0.55] },","-      ghost: { fill: 0.05, edge: 0.45 },","+      id: \"wing\", label: \"Rear wing\", subsystem: \"race-aero\",","+      prompt: \"Watch the orange arrow in the dashed box above the wing's trailing edge. Raise the flap: which way does the air leave the wing now?\",","+      answer: \"Upward. With the flap raised, the mean air direction in the box turns from about 9° down to about 4° up. Turning the air upward takes a push, and the wing feels the push back, downward. In the whole-car model that is about 34 kg more on the rear axle at 200 km/h, for about 9 kW more of the engine's power spent on drag.\",","+      more: \"Wind tunnel details\",","+      view: { pos: [-3.73, 1.88, 1.12], target: [-3.73, 0.238, 1.12] },","+      ghost: { fill: 0, edge: 0 },","       params: {","-        label: \"Wind tunnel at 200 km/h\",","-        data: \"stops/190e-evo2/cfd/\", speed: 200, start: \"flap\",","-        // Cases computed by tools/cfd (geometry.mjs CASES); flap and lip sizes illustrative.","+        label: \"Wing flap · a slice of the whole-car tunnel\",","+        data: \"stops/190e-evo2/cfd/wing/\", start: \"base\",","+        // Crop of the whole-car runs (tools/cfd/part.mjs wing; results in tools/cfd/results/wing.json); flap size illustrative.","         cases: [","-          { id: \"base\", label: \"Baseline\", say: \"The car as modelled: the reference for the other two.\" },","-          { id: \"flap\", label: \"Wing flap up\", part: [-3620, 450, 1170], say: \"The raised flap throws the air behind the car further upward: the streamlines over the boot rise and the suction around the wing deepens. The rear axle is pressed down harder, and drag grows.\" },","-          { id: \"lip\", label: \"Lip forward\", part: [960, 450, 150], say: \"The lip reaches further into the high-pressure air in front of the nose. Its effect stays within the tunnel's noise, so the readouts show –: both grids hint at slightly less downforce overall (about 16 kg) and no clear gain at the front. A lip a few centimetres deep is only one or two of the model's 28 mm blocks.\" },","+          { id: \"base\", label: \"Baseline\", say: \"Flap down: the air leaves the trailing edge heading slightly downward, about 9° below the horizontal in the box.\" },","+          { id: \"flap\", label: \"Flap raised\", say: \"Flap raised: in the same box the air now rises, about 4° upward, and the paths behind the wing bend up with it.\" },","         ],","       },","       talk: [","-        \"The racing car's rear wing has an upper flap that can be raised, and its front lip has two positions. Teams set them for each circuit: more downforce for fast bends, less drag for long straights.\",","-        \"Downforce presses the tyres onto the road. More load gives more grip in a bend, so the car can corner faster; the price is drag, which costs speed on the straight that follows.\",","-        \"Where the load lands matters as much as how much there is. Load at the rear keeps the back of the car stable; load at the front helps the car turn in. The arrows show the change at each axle, worked out from the whole car's lift and pitching moment.\",","-        \"The flared arches make room for wide tyres. Trimming them back to 15 mm outside the tyre was also tried, but that is finer than the model's 28 mm blocks: the blocky car came out unchanged, so it has no button here.\",","-        \"The model is a game mesh of the Evo II filled in as solid blocks of 28 mm, half the car with a mirror on the centre line, a moving road and still wheels, at a much lower Reynolds number than the real car. Its forces are far from the real car's: read the changes between cases, not the totals.\",","+        \"The racing car's rear wing carries an upper flap that teams could raise for circuits with fast bends. Mercedes-Benz names a retractable upper flap and a two-position front spoiler for the homologation car; the flap here is an illustrative size and angle, not the 1992 setting.\",","+        \"The picture is a slice 238 mm from the car's centre line, cut out of a 3-D wind tunnel of the whole car. Colour shows how fast the air moves up (red) or down (blue), as a share of the road speed; grey is solid: the boot lid, the wing and, when raised, the flap.\",","+        \"The orange arrow is the mean direction of the air in the dashed box, over 48 of the model's 28 mm cells. On a coarser 40 mm grid the box holds 12 cells and the direction turns from about 2° down to 6° up: both grids agree on the change, not on its size.\",","+        \"In the whole-car model, raising the flap adds about 34 kg of load on the rear axle at 200 km/h (25 to 43 kg on its two grids) and about 9 kW of drag power (4 to 14 kW); the front axle hardly changes. More rear load is grip and stability in fast bends; the drag is speed lost on the straights.\",","+        \"The model is a game mesh of the Evo II filled with 28 mm blocks: half the car with a mirror on the centre line, a moving road, wheels held still, and a Reynolds number about 300 times smaller than the real car's. Read the change between the two settings, not the values.\",","       ],","     },","     {","-      id: \"aero\", label: \"Road car on the autobahn\", subsystem: \"aero\",","-      view: { pos: [3.4, -6.2, 2.4], target: [-1.3, 0, 0.5] },","-      ghost: { fill: 0.06, edge: 0.5 },","+      id: \"splitter\", label: \"Front splitter\", subsystem: \"race-aero\",","+      prompt: \"The lip under the bumper slides forward. Follow the two air paths: one rides up over the nose, one runs under the car. Move the lip forward and watch where they divide.\",","+      answer: \"SPLITTER_ANSWER\",","+      more: \"Wind tunnel details\",","+      view: { pos: [0.84, 1.5, 0.2], target: [0.84, 0, 0.2] },","+      ghost: { fill: 0, edge: 0 },","       params: {","-        label: \"Full power on the autobahn\",","-        shapes: [","-          { id: \"evo2\", label: \"Evolution II body kit\", cd: 0.29, area: 2.05, mass: 1420, crr: 0.013, eta: 0.82, ps: 235, quoted: 250 },   // area, mass, crr, eta illustrative","-          { id: \"base\", label: \"2.3-16 body\", cd: 0.32, area: 1.95, mass: 1380, crr: 0.013, eta: 0.82, ps: 185, quoted: 230 },","+        label: \"Front lip · a section through the nose\",","+        data: \"stops/190e-evo2/cfd/splitter/\", start: \"in\",","+        // 2-D section from the traced centre-plane profile (tools/cfd/part.mjs splitter); lip travel and thickness illustrative.","+        cases: [","+          { id: \"in\", label: \"Lip in\", say: \"SPLITTER_IN\" },","+          { id: \"forward\", label: \"Lip forward\", say: \"SPLITTER_FORWARD\" },","         ],","-        power: { min: 100, max: 400 }, vmax: 300, nose: [935, 400], wheel: [-2665, 0],","-        time: 3,","-        // Centre-plane section for the 2-D wind tunnel, traced from the stop photo (a true side view) through its fitted camera","-        // (tools/profile-traces/190e-evo2.json); the rear wing is left out and the underside flat. Within about ±10 cm.","-        flow: {","-          x0: 2300, x1: -8200, top: 3500, cell: 50, show: [-6800, 2600], rake: [100, 2300, 22],","-          profile: [[935, 260], [915, 440], [870, 560], [798, 618], [435, 674], [48, 744], [-337, 814], [-530, 972], [-817, 1168], [-1102, 1305],","-            [-1575, 1347], [-2049, 1356], [-2429, 1330], [-2671, 1203], [-2918, 1017], [-3117, 877], [-3407, 844], [-3696, 816], [-3760, 700], [-3800, 450],","-            [-3790, 200], [-3700, 120], [850, 120], [920, 170]],","-        },","       },","-      talk: [","-        \"Touring-car racing rules required a road version of every racing car: 502 Evolution IIs were built in 1990 so that the DTM car could race with its body kit.\",","-        \"The front spoiler, wide wheel arches, sill panels and adjustable rear wing were shaped in the wind tunnel as one system. The result: a drag coefficient of about 0.29 against the 2.3-16's 0.32, and much less lift at speed.\",","-        \"The power needed to push through the air grows with the cube of the speed. With 235 hp the road car reaches about 250 km/h.\",","-        \"This is Klaus Ludwig's DTM car of 1992; the race version had well over 350 hp. Its rear wing adds downforce at the cost of drag.\",","-        \"The wind tunnel shows the slice along the car's centre line. Under Pressure the air presses on the nose and the windscreen and speeds up over the roof; behind the boot it breaks away into a wake that pulls the car back. The rear wing is left out of the slice.\",","-      ],","+      talk: [\"SPLITTER_TALK\"],","+    },","+    {","+      id: \"wheel-arch\", label: \"Front wheel arch\", subsystem: \"race-aero\",","+      prompt: \"Seen from above at hub height: the bumper corner, the front tyre in its flared arch, the door behind. Tap Follow the air and trace one path from in front of the car.\",","+      answer: \"ARCH_ANSWER\",","+      more: \"Wind tunnel details\",","+      view: { pos: [-0.1, 1.0, 3.6], target: [-0.1, 1.0, 0.34] },","+      ghost: { fill: 0, edge: 0 },","+      params: {","+        label: \"Front corner · seen from above at hub height\",","+        data: \"stops/190e-evo2/cfd/wheel-arch/\", start: \"fitted\", follow: true,","+        // Local 3-D run of the front corner (tools/cfd/part.mjs evo-arch, tools/cfd/corner.mjs).","+        cases: [{ id: \"fitted\", label: \"Flared arch\", say: \"ARCH_SAY\" }],","+      },","+      talk: [\"ARCH_TALK\"],","     },","     {","       id: \"engine\", label: \"Racing engine\", subsystem: \"inline-engine\","]}]}],"moreFiles":0,"changedFiles":["/Users/kunabel/workspace/MercedesExperience/stops/190e-evo2/car.js"]}},"serverClassifierContext":{"request":"f7bee542-25d8-4f01-842d-0a7c47df6359","context":{"git_state":{"cwd":"/Users/kunabel/workspace/MercedesExperience","root":"/Users/kunabel/workspace/MercedesExperience","branch":"main","default_branch":null,"status":null,"visibility":{"origin":{"host":"github.com","remote":"abelkun