{"parentUuid":"0c2d0df1-ef59-4de8-9340-8395057c356f","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011ChQMpvTU1ndAWwXLj2i7w","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01MGT7X5LVtNgCW2kcoFFanF","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"node - <<'EOF'\nconst fs=require(\"fs\"),f=\"stops/w196r/car.js\";let s=fs.readFileSync(f,\"utf8\");\nconst rep=(a,b)=>{if(!s.includes(a))throw new Error(\"missing: \"+a.slice(0,70));s=s.replace(a,b)};\nrep(`      prompt: \"Seen from above at hub height: the front tyre and the body beside it. Switch between the two bodies and watch the slow air behind the wheel at the orange line.\",\n      answer: \"WHEEL_ANSWER\",`,\n`      prompt: \"Seen from above at hub height: the front tyre, the body beside it and a dashed line across the air behind the wheel. Switch bodies: how much slow air (blue) crosses the line?\",\n      answer: \"Behind the bare tyre, a band of slow, churned air about WIDTH_EXP cm wide trails downstream. With the wheel enclosed, the slow air at the same line is only a layer about WIDTH_ENC cm thick on the body's skin. The wider body pushes more air aside, yet leaves far less churned air behind the wheel: the streamliner's advantage on the fast straights of Reims and Monza. In corners its drivers paid for it, unable to see the front wheels to place them.\",`);\nrep(`          { id: \"enclosed\", label: \"Enclosed\", say: \"WHEEL_ENCLOSED\" },\n          { id: \"exposed\", label: \"Exposed\", say: \"WHEEL_EXPOSED\" },`,\n`          { id: \"enclosed\", label: \"Enclosed\", say: \"Enclosed: the tyre turns in its own wheelhouse and the air flows round the smooth body, faster beside it. At the orange mark the slow air is a layer about WIDTH_ENC cm thick on the skin.\" },\n          { id: \"exposed\", label: \"Exposed\", say: \"Exposed: the air meets the bare tyre and leaves a band of slow air about WIDTH_EXP cm wide behind it, a little wider than the tyre itself.\" },`);\nrep(`      talk: [\"WHEEL_TALK\"],`,`      talk: [\n        \"For the new 2.5-litre Formula 1 of 1954, Mercedes planned two bodies from the start: this streamlined one for very fast circuits, and a classic one with open wheels.\",\n        \"It won first time out, at Reims on 4 July 1954: Fangio ahead of Kling. Two weeks later, on the tight Silverstone circuit, the drivers could not place the enclosed front wheels precisely in the corners and finished only 4th and 7th.\",\n        \"In 1955 the streamliner raced only once, at Monza with its new steep banking, where Fangio won. The M 196 engine, a straight eight with direct injection and desmodromic valves, now gave 290 hp at 8,500 rpm.\",\n        \"The picture is a level slice at hub height through a 3-D wind tunnel of the front corner, computed ahead of time. Colour is the air's speed against the road speed; grey is solid, body and tyre cut through. The orange mark shows where, on the dashed line 36 cm behind the tyre, the air moves slower than 80 % of the road speed.\",\n        \"Both bodies were computed with the same tyre, road, speed and starting points for the paths, on grids of 24 and 16 mm cells; GRID_NOTE The tyre stands still over a moving road (a spinning tyre needs a finer model), the suspension arms are left out and the Reynolds number is about a thousandth of the real car's: read the difference between the bodies, not the values.\",\n        \"A separate straight-line estimate, with assumed drag coefficients (0.5 enclosed, 0.7 open), frontal areas and masses, puts the streamliner about 20 km/h faster at the same 290 hp. No top speed is quoted for either body, so this is an illustration, not a figure.\",\n      ],`);\nfs.writeFileSync(f,s);\nEOF\ngrep -E \"===|window\" tools/cfd/out/logs/3d.log | tail -2; grep -h window tools/cfd/out/logs/split*.log | tail -3","description":"Write the W 196 R wheel texts with numbers to confirm"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_se