{"parentUuid":"15c48fb1-b882-4197-b8b5-93e487825863","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"520f657a-52b6-42e2-ac69-925bae4d884a","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Eu1E4M9iW4NVLJ8bFko7x6","type":"tool_result","content":"77:      prompt: \"Seen from above at hub height: the bumper corner, the front tyre in its flared arch, the door behind. Tap Follow the air and trace one path from in front of the car.\",\n78:      answer: \"It starts 78 cm from the car's centre line, in line with the tyre, and the bumper corner turns it outward. It never comes back in: by the dashed line, 60 cm behind the axle, it runs about ARCH_SHIFT cm further out, well clear of the door. The flared arch is the widest point of the car, and along the flank behind it the air moves slower (green and blue) than in the fast stream outside (red).\",\n86:        cases: [{ id: \"fitted\", label: \"Flared arch\", say: \"This path starts in line with the tyre, just in front of the bumper corner. The corner and the flared arch push it about ARCH_SHIFT cm outward; it keeps that line past the door.\" }],\n89:        \"The Evolution II's arches were widened so that wider wheels and tyres would fit under the body: on the road car about 225/50 ZR 17 tyres on 8.5-inch rims.\",\n90:        \"The picture is a level slice at hub height through a 3-D wind tunnel of the front corner, computed ahead of time from the same game mesh as the rear wing. Colour is the air's speed against the road speed; grey is solid, body and tyre cut through.\",\n91:        \"The tyre stands still in its carved-out wheelhouse, 48 mm above a moving road; the body is cut flat 1 m up and the half car is mirrored at the centre line. ARCH_GRIDS\",\n92:        \"There is no second arch here to compare with. The mesh's flare already sits within a centimetre of the tyre's outer face, so a narrower arch could not cover the tyre: the lesson is where the air goes, not what the flare is worth.\",\n126:    \"Front wheel arch close-up: a local 3-D tunnel of the front corner from the game mesh (body clipped flat at 1 m, wheelhouse carved round the tyre, tyre held still 48 mm above the moving road, half car mirrored), Reynolds number 10⁴ on the tyre diameter\",","is_error":false}]},"uuid":"13b986e3-3ea6-4d5f-96b5-f4297397925b","timestamp":"2026-11-25T21:39:43.849Z","toolUseResult":{"stdout":"77:      prompt: \"Seen from above at hub height: the bumper corner, the front tyre in its flared arch, the door behind. Tap Follow the air and trace one path from in front of the car.\",\n78:      answer: \"It starts 78 cm from the car's centre line, in line with the tyre, and the bumper corner turns it outward. It never comes back in: by the dashed line, 60 cm behind the axle, it runs about ARCH_SHIFT cm further out, well clear of the door. The flared arch is the widest point of the car, and along the flank behind it the air moves slower (green and blue) than in the fast stream outside (red).\",\n86:        cases: [{ id: \"fitted\", label: \"Flared arch\", say: \"This path starts in line with the tyre, just in front of the bumper corner. The corner and the flared arch push it about ARCH_SHIFT cm outward; it keeps that line past the door.\" }],\n89:        \"The Evolution II's arches were widened so that wider wheels and tyres would fit under the body: on the road car about 225/50 ZR 17 tyres on 8.5-inch rims.\",\n90:        \"The picture is a level slice at hub height through a 3-D wind tunnel of the front corner, computed ahead of time from the same game mesh as the rear wing. Colour is the air's speed against the road speed; grey is solid, body and tyre cut through.\",\n91:        \"The tyre stands still in its carved-out wheelhouse, 48 mm above a moving road; the body is cut flat 1 m up and the half car is mirrored at the centre line. ARCH_GRIDS\",\n92:        \"There is no second arch here to compare with. The mesh's flare already sits within a centimetre of the tyre's outer face, so a narr