son for each part, compact fields, and the exact claim each supports |\n43:1. From the repo root check for `tools/cfd/evo2.glb` and `tools/cfd/out/{base,flap}-{28,40}.{json,bin,solid}`. The mesh and raw outputs are ignored by Git. This checkout has them. A clean checkout can run the published site from its committed assets. To regenerate a missing reference volume with the original mesh, use e.g. `node tools/cfd/run.mjs flap 28 9000`. Repeat only the missing case/grid pairs. Use the reco\n70:Rows run from bottom to top. Advancing a column always decreases vehicle x. Advancing a row increases z for `xz` or y for `xy`. The origin is the **centre of the first cell**. Use vertical velocity divided by inlet speed for the wing, pressure coefficient for the splitter, and mean-flow speed divided by inlet speed for the wheel views. Each binary is exactly `width * height` bytes in row-major order. Reserve 255 f\n72:1. Start with `lib/model/part-field.js` and `tests/unit/part-field.test.mjs` for the pure format/mapping logic used by the exporter and player. A tiny asymmetric 3 × 2 fixture should catch a left/right mirror, row flip, misplaced half cell, bad byte count or solid decoded as a field value. Check both plane orientations, endpoints of the colour range, and the generated manifest’s case IDs against the stop config.\n81:### C. Compute the local wing and splitter\n83:**Repeatable offline runs.** Add the local cases behind `part.mjs`. A useful command contract is `node tools/cfd/part.mjs <wing|splitter|w196-wheel|evo-arch> <solve|export>`. These are proposed commands to implement. Each `solve` runs the component’s specified cases/grids into its own ignored output directory. Each `export` validates the complete case set, then publishes the compact display assets to its stop fold\n87:**Splitter.** Draw two closed nose sections from the same bumper profile and ride height. Extend the lip longitudinally. The current 40 mm travel and 25 mm thickness are illustrative. Start at 5 mm per cell, which gives eight cells of travel and five through the plate, then repeat at finer spacing, for example 2.5 mm. Use `rasterize()` and `createTunnel()` from the existing 2-D model in Node. Allow fluid above and\n101:- **Stop data.** Use Evo component IDs `wing`, `splitter`, `wheel-arch`, then its existing `engine`. Use W 196 R component IDs `wheel`, then its existing `engine`. Point all four flow steps at `race-aero` with distinct data folders and component-specific `view`, `prompt`, `answer`, `more` and `talk`. Use `params.data` for the precomputed assets. The existing `params.flow` belongs to the live tunnel and its tests","is_error":false}]},"uuid":"da0d06d3-ad87-43da-b08f-d1164ad0caa2","timestamp":"2026-11-25T22:38:04.944Z","toolUseResult":{"stdout":"3:Replace the Evo’s two whole-car airflow views with three experiments, **rear wing → front splitter → front wheel arch**. Replace the W 196 R centre-line tunnel with one **enclosed / exposed front wheel** experiment. The museum photo locates each part. Scrolling brings it into close-up. One tap should reveal something the air does, even with the animation paused.\n11:| Compared with the Evo baseline, the 28 mm half-car mask changes **90 cells for the flap, 30 for the lip, zero for the arch**. At 40 mm the counts are **39, 26, zero**. All four published force-change flags for the lip are false. | The archived wing is a usable visual reference. The splitter needs a finer grid. The arch needs geometry that actually includes the flare and wheelhouse opening. Camera magnification p\n14:| The live solver uses a 2-D lattice, one side-profile mask, a moving road and stationary solid walls. The offline 3-D runner uses uniform inflow, a mirrored half-car, a simplified floor, stationary stepped tyres and a much lower Reynolds number than the vehicle. Its browser export contains two fixed scalar slices and pretraced paths. | Reuse the 2-D solver offline for the splitter and the 3-D kernel for the wing \n23:| **E