{"parentUuid":"46c1a057-a678-46f4-989b-3b917983f0a2","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"b3f8a1dc-7f2b-4042-8621-c991e0a01150","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01VUVZnq7pf8feivoKpsuLnS","type":"tool_result","content":"12:  lede: \"Klaus Ludwig's DTM car carries a rear wing with an adjustable upper flap, a front lip that slides forward and flared wheel arches. Three close-ups of wind tunnels computed ahead of time show what each part does to the air around it.\",\n50:        \"The racing car's rear wing carries an upper flap that teams could raise for circuits with fast bends. Mercedes-Benz names a retractable upper flap and a two-position front spoiler for the homologation car; the flap here is an illustrative size and \n59:      prompt: \"The lip under the bumper slides forward. Follow the two air paths: one rides up over the nose, one runs under the car. Move the lip forward: what is above it now, and what is below?\",\n75:        \"Mercedes-Benz describes a front spoiler with two positions for the Evolution II. The lip's 4 cm of travel and 25 mm thickness here are illustrative.\",\n126:  note: \"The road Evolution II's body kit (front spoiler, wide wheel arches, sill panels and adjustable rear wing) was shaped in the wind tunnel as one system: a drag coefficient of about 0.29 against the 2.3-16's 0.32, much less lift at speed, and about 2\n140:    { label: \"Mercedes-Benz: the adjustable aerodynamics of the Evolution II\", url: \"https://media.mercedes-benz.com/en/article/4e61dcd8-fd75-47ca-830c-056734a165c2\" },\n136:  sources: [\n137-    { label: \"Wikipedia: Mercedes-Benz 190 E 2.5-16 Evolution II\", url: \"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mercedes-Benz_190_E_2.5-16_Evolution_II\" },\n138-    { label: \"Mercedes-Benz: the road and racing engines of the 190 E 2.5-16 Evolution II\", url: \"https://media.mercedes-benz.com/en/article/25dd00e4-5ef9-4c2f-b12c-55169f795d4d\" },\n139-    { label: \"3-D model used for the wind tunnel: “M