{"parentUuid":"226633e1-8d4c-4f83-aaff-c55b8151cfa2","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011ChQUAASZzo64deGEcMR6G","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Not quite. The flap and the lip are both adjustable, but neither moved while the car was driving: these were static settings, not active aerodynamics like a modern DRS.\n\n- **Rear wing:** the upper flap could be raised or retracted. Evo magazine calls the trailing edge \"manually adjustable\", which means it was set on the car rather than worked by the driver.\n- **Front spoiler:** the Mercedes-Benz article the stop cites says it \"was adjustable in two stages in the longitudinal direction\". So it has two fixed positions, further back or further forward.\n\nSo the similarity you noticed is right in one sense: both are settings chosen for a circuit. The difference is the range, from what the sources say: the front spoiler has two fixed stages, while no source gives a number of positions for the rear flap.\n\nTwo caveats from that Mercedes article:\n- It doesn't say how either part was adjusted.\n- It describes the road homologation car. Whether the DTM car used the same settings isn't stated.\n\nThe stop's text already handles the wing well: \"an upper flap that teams could raise for circuits with fast bends\". But \"a front lip that slides forward\" in the lede could suggest it moves while driving. I can add a sentence to the splitter step, in English and Hungarian: both parts are set before running, and neither is described as moving while driving. Do you want that?\n\nSources:\n- [Mercedes-Benz: the adjustable aerodynamics of the Evolution II](https://media.mercedes-benz.com/en/article/4e61dcd8-fd75-47ca-830c-056734a165c2)\n- [evo: the Mercedes 190E 2.5-16 Evo II's bodykit](https://www.evo.co.uk/features/18091/the-mercedes-190e-25-16-evo-iis-bodykit-evo-art-of-speed)\n- [Octane: Mercedes-Benz 190E Evo II – Earning its wings](https://www.octane-magazine.com/articles/features/mercedes-benz-190e-evo-ii-earning-its-wings/)"}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"end_turn","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2086,"cache_read_input_tokens":228772,"output_tokens":1282,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":607},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":2086,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"global","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":1282,"cache_read_input_tokens":228772,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2086,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":2086},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard","fallback_credit":null},"input_transformations":[{"type":"thinking_dropped","path":"messages.2.content.0","reason":"prefix_binding_mismatch"},{"type":"thinking_dropped","path":"messages.4.content.0","reason":"prefix_binding_mismatch"},{"type":"thinking_dropped","path":"messages.4.content.1","reason":"prefix_binding_mismatch"},{"type":"thinking_dropped","path":"messages.6.content.0","reason":"prefix_binding_mismatch"},{"type":"thinking_dropped","path":"messages.6.content.1","reason":"prefix_binding_mismatch"},{"type":"thinking_dropped","path":"messages.10.content.0","reason":"prefix_binding_mismatch"},{"type":"thinking_dropped","path":"messages.12.content.0","reason":"prefix_binding_mismatch"},{"type":"thinking_dropped","path":"messages.14.content.0","reason":"prefix_binding_mismatch"},{"type":"thinking_dropped","path":"messages.19.content.0","reason":"prefix_binding_mismatch"},{"type":"thinking_dropped","path":"messages.21.content.0","reason":"prefix_binding_mismatch"},{"type":"thinking_dropped","path":"messages.21.content.1","reason":"prefix_binding_mismatch"},{"type":"thinking_dropped","path":"messages.23.content.0","reason":"prefix_binding_mismatch"},{"type":"thinking_dropped","path":"messages.25.content.0","reason":