{"parentUuid":"3a4c9735-7684-429d-aebe-9f010ea3356e","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"ec09b693-1520-4ce3-9d06-d5a5710775a6","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_016eqEemUNZPDxCd6335SnsL","type":"tool_result","content":"51:        \"Both bodies were computed with the same tyre, road, speed and starti","is_error":false}]},"uuid":"ee1a9a44-1c15-4285-b980-21bdded9cef4","timestamp":"2026-11-26T00:33:54.415Z","toolUseResult":{"stdout":"51:        \"Both bodies were computed with the same tyre, road, speed and starti","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false,"bashEditDiff":{"files":[{"filePath":"/Users/kunabel/workspace/MercedesExperience/stops/w196r/car.js","hunks":[{"oldStart":29,"oldLines":8,"newStart":29,"newLines":8,"lines":["   components: [","     {","       id: \"wheel\", label: \"Front wheel\", subsystem: \"race-aero\",","-      prompt: \"Seen from above at hub height: the front tyre, the body beside it and a dashed line across the air behind the wheel. Switch bodies: how much slow air (blue) crosses the line?\",","-      answer: \"Behind the bare tyre, a band of slow, churned air about 23 cm wide trails downstream. With the wheel enclosed, the slow air at the same line is only a layer about 4 cm thick on the body's skin. The wider body pushes more air aside, yet leaves far less churned air behind the wheel: the streamliner's advantage on the fast straights of Reims and Monza. In corners its drivers paid for it, unable to see the front wheels to place them.\",","+      prompt: \"Seen from above at hub height: the front tyre, the body beside it and a dashed line across the air behind the wheel. Switch bodies: what happens to the air just behind the wheel?\",","+      answer: \"Behind the bare tyre, the air in a patch the width of the wheel loses more than half its speed: a trail of slow, churned air. With the wheel enclosed, that patch lies inside the body: the tyre's churned air stays in its wheelhouse, and outside the air runs along the smooth skin. The streamliner is wider, yet shows the air no bare wheel: its advantage on the fast straights of Reims and Monza. In corners its drivers paid for it, unable to see the front wheels to place them.\",","       more: \"Wind tunnel details\",","       view: { pos: [0.1, 1.3, 4.1], target: [0.1, 0.75, 0.344] },","       ghost: { fill: 0, edge: 0 },"]},{"oldStart":39,"oldLines":15,"newStart":39,"newLines":15,"lines":["         data: \"stops/w196r/cfd/wheel/\", start: \"enclosed\",","         // Local 3-D runs of the front corner (tools/cfd/part.mjs w196-wheel, tools/cfd/corner.mjs); body sizes assumed.","         cases: [","-          { id: \"enclosed\", label: \"Enclosed\", say: \"Enclosed: the tyre turns in its own wheelhouse and the air flows round the smooth body, faster beside it. At the orange mark the slow air is a layer about 4 cm thick on the skin.\" },","-          { id: \"exposed\", label: \"Exposed\", say: \"Exposed: the air meets the bare tyre and leaves a band of slow air about 23 cm wide behind it, a little wider than the tyre itself.\" },","+          { id: \"enclosed\", label: \"Enclosed\", say: \"Enclosed: the tyre turns in its own wheelhouse, and the air flows along the smooth body. The patch behind the wheel is inside the body.\" },","+          { id: \"exposed\", label: \"Exposed\", say: \"Exposed: the air meets the bare tyre and leaves a band of slow air behind it, a little wider than the tyre itself.\" },","         ],","       },","       talk: [","         \"For the new 2.5-litre Formula 1 of 1954, Mercedes planned two bodies from the start: this streamlined one for very fast circuits, and a classic one with open wheels.\",","         \"It won first time out, at Reims on 4 July 1954: Fangio ahead of Kling. Two weeks later, on the tight Silverstone circuit, the drivers could not place the enclosed front wheels precisely in the corners and finished only 4th and 7th.\",","         \"In 1955 the streamliner raced only once, at Monza with its new steep banking, where Fangio won. The M 196 engine, a straight eight with direct injection and desmodromic valves, now gave 290 hp at 8,500 rpm.\",","-        \"The picture is a level slice at hub height through a 3-D wind tunnel of the front corner, computed ahead of time. Colour is the air's speed against the road speed; grey is solid, body and tyre cut through. The orange mark shows where, on the dashed line 36 cm behind the tyre, the air moves slower than 80 % of the road speed.\",","+        \"The picture is a level slice at hub height through a 3-D wind tunnel of the front corner, computed ahead of time. Colour is the air's speed against the road speed; grey is solid, body and tyre cut through. With the wheel exposed, the orange mark shows where, on the dashed line 36 cm behind the tyre, the air moves slower than 80 % of the road speed.\",","         \"Both bodies were computed with the same tyre, road, speed and starting points for the paths, on grids of 24 and 16 mm cells; GRID_NOTE The tyre stands still over a moving road (a spinning tyre needs a finer model), the suspension arms are left out and the Reynolds number is about a thousandth of the real car's: read the difference between the bodies, not the values.\",","         \"A separate straight-line estimate, with assumed drag coefficients (0.5 enclosed, 0.7 open), frontal areas and masses, puts the streamliner about 20 km/h faster at the same 290 hp. No top speed is quoted for either body, so this is an illustration, not a figure.\",","       ],"]}]}],"moreFiles":0,"changedFiles":["/Users/kunabel/workspace/MercedesExperience/stops/w196r/car.js"]}},"serverClassifierContext":{"request":"c61d2d9b-182c-484f-9a5a-be761a5a71ec","context":{"git_state":{"cwd":"/Users/kunabel/workspace/MercedesExperience","root":"/Users/kunabel/workspace/MercedesExperience","branch":"main","default_branch":null,"status":null,"visibility":{"origin":{"host":"github.com","remote":"abel