{"parentUuid":"ab9ee680-0203-4951-a8df-cd973d408139","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"ec09b693-1520-4ce3-9d06-d5a5710775a6","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Mtu5HfeswEgBrRgbwC3A32","type":"tool_result","content":[{"type":"text","text":"[computer:key] Executed cmd+shift+r (hard reload page)"},{"type":"text","text":"[computer:key] Executed cmd+shift+r (hard reload page)"},{"type":"text","text":"[computer:wait] Waited for 4 seconds"},{"type":"text","text":"[javascript_tool:javascript_exec] [\n  \"Seen from above at hub height: the front tyre, the body beside it and a dashed line across the air behind the wheel. Switch bodies: what happens to the air just behind the wheel?\",\n  \"Enclosed: the tyre turns in its own wheelhouse, and the air flows along the smooth body. The patch behind the wheel is inside the body.\",\n  \"\",\n  \"Behind the bare tyre, the air in a patch the width of the wheel loses about half its speed: a trail of slow, churned air. With the wheel enclosed, that patch lies inside the body: the tyre's churned air stays in its wheelhouse, and outside the air runs along the smooth skin. The streamliner is wider, yet shows the air no bare wheel: its advantage on the fast straights of Reims and Monza. In corners its drivers paid for it, unable to see the front wheels to place them.\"\n]"},{"type":"text","text":"[computer:screenshot] Successfully captured screenshot (735x374, jpeg) - ID: ss_3601ak4zv — 0.5-scale view; coordinate frame: 1470x748."},{"type":"image","source":{"type":"base64","media_type":"image/jpeg","data":""}},{"type":"text","text":"\n\nTab Context:\n- Executed on tabId: 1061651925\n- Available tabs:\n  • tabId 1061651922: \"Mercedes-Benz 190 E 2.5-16 Evolution II · Museum tour\" (\"http://localhost:8765/stop.html?s=190e-evo2&debug\")\n  • tabId 1061651925: \"Mercedes-Benz W 196 R streamliner · Museum tour\" (\"http://localhost:8765/stop.html?s=w196r&debug\")"}]}]},"uuid":"ddda3637-93e4-491f-98bc-4ad040